/**

@page page_exahype_modelling_pde_solver Modelling the PDE solver

\tableofcontents

This page discusses how a user describes *what* their solver should compute,
i.e. the mathematical model, as opposed to *how* it is eventually computed
(discussed on the @ref page_exahype_numerical_scheme "adding a numerical scheme" page).
Modelling is entirely done through exahype2.api.Solver and stays purely
descriptive, in line with the @ref page_exahype_design_philosophy "design philosophy"
that keeps the *what* free of any *how*.

ExaHyPE 2 solves partial differential equations of the form

@f$
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial t} + \nabla . F(Q, \nabla Q) + B(Q) . \nabla Q= S(Q) + \sum_{i = 1}^{n_{ps}} \delta_i
@f$

and users model an instance of this equation by creating an exahype2.api.Solver
(or a concrete subclass thereof, such as a Finite Volume or ADER-DG solver) and
configuring two things on it: the unknowns and the individual PDE terms.


# Unknowns and material parameters

Every solver is constructed with a name and an unknowns specification. The
latter can be given either as a plain integer (just the multiplicity) or as a
dictionary mapping a variable name onto its multiplicity, e.g.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver = exahype2.api.Solver(
  name     = "MyEulerSolver",
  unknowns = {"rho": 1, "j": 3, "E": 1},
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The unknowns form the data vector Q per mesh entry (Finite Volume voxel, DG
degree of freedom, ...) and are subject to the actual PDE formulation above. If a
dictionary is used, the generated abstract solver hosts a struct Variables, so
user code can address Q's entries by name instead of by raw offset.

Quantities which may vary in space but are not evolved by the PDE terms are
material parameters. A solver can have an arbitrary number of sets of material parameters
(@ref exahype2.api.MaterialParameters), each of them holding an arbitrary number of
(vector-valued) variables in a cell data object of its own. A set is attached via
exahype2.api.Solver.add_material_parameters(), and the first solver which a set
is attached to owns it, i.e. initialises it. Further solvers can attach the same set
and share it. Behind each Q (and deltaQ), the signature of every PDE term of a
solver with material parameters hosts one pointer per attached set. Solvers without
material parameters keep their signatures.


# PDE terms

Q, F, B, S and the point sources @f$ \delta_i @f$ from the equation above each
correspond to one exahype2.api.PDETerm registered with the solver via
Solver.add_pde_term(). Well-known terms - the flux (exahype2.api.Flux),
eigenvalues, the non-conservative product, algebraic/point sources, boundary
conditions, ... - ship as their own PDETerm subclasses that pin down the
C++ method name and signature; solver-specific terms can be added with the
plain PDETerm constructor.

A term's implementation is one of

- `PDETerm.USER_DEFINED`: the generated C++ method is left pure virtual for
  the user to implement by hand,
- `PDETerm.NONE` (the default): the term is switched off, i.e. this
  contribution is absent from the PDE, or
- `PDETerm.EMPTY`: the method is generated but does nothing,

or an actual C++ code snippet that becomes the method body verbatim, e.g. for
small, closed-form terms that do not warrant a hand-written implementation.

A term is thus itself a small, purely descriptive object (name, signature,
implementation), and it is only the corresponding generator
(term.generator, @see PDETerm.Generator) that later lowers it into an actual
generated C++ method - the same descriptive-class-plus-generator pattern used
throughout ExaHyPE 2 (@see @ref page_exahype_design_philosophy).

*/
